{
  // Gate CI on HIGH and CRITICAL severity advisories only.
  //
  // WHY NOT all severities: `npm audit` reports advisories against the entire
  // dependency graph regardless of reachability, and this tree's moderate/low
  // advisories are dominated by two unactionable classes:
  //   1. PHANTOM — `dompurify` is pulled by monaco-editor, which VENDORS its own
  //      copy (`esm/vs/base/browser/dompurify/dompurify.js`) and imports THAT,
  //      not the npm package. The npm `dompurify` is never executed; there is no
  //      reachable vulnerability and nothing to fix. The advisory DB mints new
  //      DOMPurify bypass advisories continuously (8 -> 16 in days), so a per-GHSA
  //      allowlist is an unwinnable treadmill.
  //   2. NO CLEAN FIX — transitive advisories whose only remedy is a breaking
  //      upgrade of a parent we don't control (see the accepted list below).
  //
  // Gating on all severities produced constant red CI that blocked unrelated PRs
  // and trains force-merging -- a worse security posture than green CI that fails
  // only on what is actually severe and actionable. High/critical advisories are
  // rare, warrant breaking the build, and are usually fixable. Current tree:
  // 13 high package findings, 0 critical, arising from four GHSA advisories; all
  // four high advisories are documented exceptions below. Run `npm audit` to
  // review the full moderate/low set.
  //
  // KNOWN ACCEPTED moderate/low advisories (informational; below the gate, so not
  // listed as allowlist entries -- re-triage on major dependency upgrades):
  //   - dompurify (via monaco-editor): PHANTOM, see above. No fix possible/needed.
  //   - @opentelemetry/core and ~13 sibling @opentelemetry/instrumentation-*
  //     advisories (via @sentry/node, lighthouse): dev-only -- lighthouse is used
  //     solely by the `lighthouse:smoke` script, never shipped to production.
  //     lighthouse vendors its OWN pinned `@sentry/node@9.x` internally, separate
  //     from this repo's real `@sentry/nextjs`/`@sentry/node@10.65.0` integration
  //     (already upgraded, already clean). Confirmed (2026-07) that bumping
  //     lighthouse to its current latest (13.4.0) does NOT clear this -- the
  //     vulnerable @sentry/node stays pinned inside lighthouse's own dependency
  //     tree regardless of lighthouse's own version. No fix exists until
  //     lighthouse's maintainers update that internal pin themselves.
  //     REVISIT next time lighthouse is bumped -- re-check `npm ls @sentry/node
  //     --all` for whether `node_modules/lighthouse/node_modules/@sentry/node`
  //     has moved past 10.53.1.
  //   - postcss (via next): GHSA-qx2v-qp2m-jg93, no fix. REVISIT on the next Next.js upgrade.
  //   - @ai-sdk/provider-utils (via ai): GHSA-866g-f22w-33x8 (low). REVISIT on the ai@6 migration.
  //
  // If a HIGH/critical advisory ever lands that is itself phantom/unfixable,
  // allowlist that ONE advisory here by GHSA id with a dated reason -- a rare,
  // deliberate exception, not a standing treadmill.
  "high": true,
  "allowlist": [
    // 2026-07-27 — brace-expansion via minimatch@3 in ESLint 9 tooling. The patched
    // v5 export is an object, but minimatch@3 requires a directly callable function;
    // forcing v5 into that chain makes every lint command fail with `expand is not a
    // function`. npm reports ESLint 10 as the available fix, which is an explicitly
    // deferred major upgrade. This path processes first-party lint globs only and is
    // not runtime-reachable. REVISIT with the ESLint 10 / eslint-config-next upgrade.
    "GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg",
    // 2026-07-27 — postcss via next. next@16.2.12 hard-pins `postcss: "8.4.31"` as an
    // EXACT dependency, and npm honors `overrides` only from the root project, which
    // does not propagate into the apps/web workspace's transitives. Both advisories
    // require attacker-controlled CSS or source-map input; postcss here runs at BUILD
    // time over this repo's own first-party stylesheets. REVISIT on the next Next.js
    // upgrade — check `npm view next@<new> dependencies` for the postcss pin.
    "GHSA-6g55-p6wh-862q",
    "GHSA-r28c-9q8g-f849",
    // 2026-07-27 — sharp via next. Declared by next as an OPTIONAL dependency at
    // `^0.34.5`, used only by Next's Image Optimization API. apps/web has zero
    // `next/image` imports and no `images:`/`remotePatterns` config, so sharp is never
    // loaded — the libvips CVEs are unreachable in this deployment. REVISIT if this app
    // ever adopts next/image, which would make this immediately actionable.
    "GHSA-f88m-g3jw-g9cj"
  ]
}
